        "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n<title>Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) - Mathematical Preliminaries</title>\n<script>\nMathJax = { tex: { inlineMath: [['$','$'], ['\\\\(','\\\\)']], displayMath: [['$$','$$'], ['\\\\[','\\\\]']] } };\n</script>\n<script async src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"></script>\n<style>\n  @page { size: 8.5in 11in; margin: 0; }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #fff; color: #000; }\n  body {\n    width: 8.5in;\n    min-height: 11in;\n    margin: 0 auto;\n    padding: 0.43in 0.86in 0.28in;\n    font-family: \"Times New Roman\", Times, serif;\n    font-size: 16.2px;\n    line-height: 1.25;\n  }\n  .running-header {\n    height: 0.52in;\n    display: flex;\n    justify-content: space-between;\n    align-items: flex-start;\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 15px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.8px;\n    font-variant: small-caps;\n  }\n  main { width: 100%; }\n  h1, h2 {\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    margin: 0;\n    font-weight: 700;\n    letter-spacing: 0;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    font-size: 24px;\n    line-height: 1.1;\n    margin: 0 0 0.22in;\n  }\n  h1 .num { display: inline-block; width: 0.44in; }\n  h2 {\n    font-size: 20px;\n    line-height: 1.1;\n    margin: 0.18in 0 0.18in;\n  }\n  h2 .num { display: inline-block; width: 0.48in; }\n  p { margin: 0 0 0.13in; text-align: left; }\n  .equation {\n    position: relative;\n    text-align: center;\n    margin: 0.19in 0 0.19in;\n    min-height: 0.22in;\n    font-size: 17px;\n  }\n  .equation .label {\n    position: absolute;\n    right: 0;\n    top: 0;\n  }\n  .example-equations {\n    margin: 0.37in 0 0.31in;\n    font-size: 16px;\n  }\n  .example-equations .row {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.45in;\n    column-gap: 0.1in;\n    align-items: baseline;\n    margin: 0.035in 0;\n  }\n  .example-equations .formula { text-align: center; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .example-equations .kind { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .footnote {\n    border-top: 1px solid #000;\n    width: 3.1in;\n    margin-top: 0.08in;\n    padding-top: 0.02in;\n    font-size: 10.5px;\n    line-height: 1.08;\n  }\n  .page-number {\n    text-align: center;\n    font-size: 14px;\n    margin-top: 0.08in;\n  }\n  sup { line-height: 0; }\n  i { font-style: italic; }\n  @media print {\n    body { margin: 0; }\n  }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <header class=\"running-header\">\n    <div>FIPS 197</div>\n    <div>Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)</div>\n  </header>\n\n  <main>\n    <h1><span class=\"num\">4.</span>Mathematical Preliminaries</h1>\n\n    <p>For some transformations of the AES algorithms specified in Sec. <span style=\"color:#0000ee\">5</span>, each byte in the state array is interpreted as one of the 256 elements of a finite field, also known as a Galois Field, denoted by GF(2<sup>8</sup>). <sup style=\"color:#0000ee\">1</sup></p>\n\n    <p>In order to define addition and multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>), each byte {<i>b</i><sub>7</sub> <i>b</i><sub>6</sub> <i>b</i><sub>5</sub> <i>b</i><sub>4</sub> <i>b</i><sub>3</sub> <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>} is interpreted as a polynomial, denoted by <i>b</i>(<i>x</i>), as follows:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"equation\"><i>b</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>b</i><sub>7</sub><i>x</i><sup>7</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>6</sub><i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>5</sub><i>x</i><sup>5</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>4</sub><i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>3</sub><i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>2</sub><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>b</i><sub>1</sub><i>x</i> + <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>.<span class=\"label\">(4.1)</span></div>\n\n    <p>For example, {01100011} is represented by the polynomial <i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>5</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1.</p>\n\n    <h2><span class=\"num\">4.1</span>Addition in GF(2<sup>8</sup>)</h2>\n\n    <p>In order to add two elements in the finite field GF(2<sup>8</sup>), the coefficients of the polynomials that represent the elements are added modulo 2 (i.e., with the exclusive-OR operation, denoted by ⊕), so that 1 ⊕ 1 = 0, 1 ⊕ 0 = 1, and 0 ⊕ 0 = 0.</p>\n\n    <p>Equivalently, two bytes can be added by applying the exclusive-OR operation to each pair of corresponding bits in the bytes. Thus, the sum of {<i>a</i><sub>7</sub> <i>a</i><sub>6</sub> <i>a</i><sub>5</sub> <i>a</i><sub>4</sub> <i>a</i><sub>3</sub> <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> <i>a</i><sub>1</sub> <i>a</i><sub>0</sub>} and {<i>b</i><sub>7</sub> <i>b</i><sub>6</sub> <i>b</i><sub>5</sub> <i>b</i><sub>4</sub> <i>b</i><sub>3</sub> <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>} is {<i>a</i><sub>7</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>7</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>6</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>6</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>5</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>5</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>4</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>4</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>3</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>3</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>1</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> &nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>0</sub> ⊕ <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>}. (In Section <span style=\"color:#0000ee\">5.1.4</span>, this definition is extended to words.)</p>\n\n    <p>For example, the following three representations of addition are equivalent:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"example-equations\">\n      <div class=\"row\"><div class=\"formula\">(<i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1) + (<i>x</i><sup>7</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1) = <i>x</i><sup>7</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup></div><div class=\"kind\">(polynomial)</div></div>\n      <div class=\"row\"><div class=\"formula\">{01010111} ⊕ {10000011} = {11010100}</div><div class=\"kind\">(binary)</div></div>\n      <div class=\"row\"><div class=\"formula\">{57} ⊕ {83} = {d4}</div><div class=\"kind\">(hexadecimal).</div></div>\n      <span class=\"label\" style=\"float:right\">(4.2)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Because the coefficients of the polynomials are reduced modulo 2, the coefficient 1 is equivalent to the coefficient −1, so addition is equivalent to subtraction. For example, <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> represents the same finite field element as <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> − <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, −<i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, and −<i>x</i><sup>4</sup> − <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>. Similarly, the sum of any element with itself is the zero element.</p>\n\n    <h2><span class=\"num\">4.2</span>Multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>)</h2>\n\n    <p>The symbol • denotes multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>). Conceptually, this multiplication is defined on two bytes in two steps: 1) the two polynomials that represent the bytes are multiplied as polynomials, and 2) the resulting polynomial is reduced modulo the following fixed polynomial:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"equation\"><i>m</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i><sup>8</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1.<span class=\"label\">(4.3)</span></div>\n\n    <p>Within both steps, the individual coefficients of the polynomials are reduced modulo 2.</p>\n  </main>\n\n  <footer>\n    <div class=\"footnote\"><sup>1</sup>Information about the properties of finite fields can be found in textbooks, such as Michael Artin’s <i>Algebra</i> <span style=\"color:#0000ee\">[4]</span>.</div>\n    <div class=\"page-number\">8</div>\n  </footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n"
